maria: Hey devex — running into an issue importing the JS SDK in Next.js middleware (Edge runtime). Next build succeeds but at runtime I get `ReferenceError: require is not defined` or `process is not defined`. Anyone hit this?

aj: Yup, seen it. Short version: the SDK currently publishes a CJS entry that some bundlers pick up. Edge runtimes require ESM + no Node globals. Quick workaround is to import a dedicated ESM/edge entry or use dynamic import inside the middleware. Example we've used: 
```js
export default async function middleware(req) {
  const { createClient } = await import('redwood-inference/edge')
  const client = createClient({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY, fetch: globalThis.fetch })
  // ...
}
```
This avoids bundling the CJS main file.

kevin: Also note a few gotchas: Next's Edge bundler strips some Node envs so don't rely on `process` at runtime. Avoid timers and non-web crypto APIs. For TypeScript you'll often hit `Cannot find name 'fetch'` or similar — add `"lib": ["DOM", "WebWorker"]` (or at least `dom`) in tsconfig so edge globals are available.

alexa: We added an `edge` subpath in one of our sample apps to keep things tidy. See https://github.com/redwood-inference/sample-apps/pull/123 (small example). The pattern is: ship a minimal ESM build that only references web-standard APIs and export typed entrypoints for edge.

maria: I tried importing `redwood-inference/edge` and adjusted tsconfig libs, and it improved but now I hit `TypeError: The requested module '.../dist/index.mjs' does not provide an export named 'default'` when using `import client from 'redwood-inference/edge'`. Switched to named import `import { createClient } from 'redwood-inference/edge'` and that fixed it.

aj: Good catch — we should recommend named exports for SDK in the middleware samples to avoid ESM default interop problems across bundlers. Also the root cause is conditional exports in package.json; bundlers sometimes resolve the wrong subpath. In the SDK we should add an explicit `exports` map for `./edge` with an `import` entry pointing to a small edge ESM bundle and include corresponding `types` entry. Example package.json snippet: 
```json
"exports": {
  "./edge": {
    "import": "./dist/edge/index.mjs"
  }
}
```

kevin: tiny tip for TS users: if you still get missing types, add a `types` entry that references `dist/edge/index.d.ts` and in that file export `export declare function createClient(opts: any): any;` — minimal but helpful.

edge-bot: deploy-bot: canary build for sdk-edge-shim passed — published `@rdw/sdk-edge@0.3.1-canary.1`.

kevin: :thumbsup: I'll open a short DX doc for devex with: recommended import lines, tsconfig snippet, and the dynamic-import fallback for older Next versions.

maria: Please also note in the doc: prefer named exports (`createClient`) and call out that `globalThis.fetch` should be forwarded into the SDK if the runtime doesn't expose some variants.

aj: I'll draft the PR and file a bundler issue for Next 13.5 picking CJS incorrectly. If anyone wants to test the canary: `npm i @rdw/sdk-edge@0.3.1-canary.1` and import from `'redwood-inference/edge'`.

maria: Thanks — that unblock helped our middleware auth flow.

(ended with a short audit note: kevin will add doc + alexa will add sample app change + aj will open bundler bug and SDK PR)
